<resources>
    <!-- Refresh rate.  Without an override AOSP leaves config_defaultRefreshRate at
         60, which DisplayModeDirector turns into a global [0, 60] Hz render vote, so
         scrolling and animations ran at 60 fps on the 144 Hz panel.  The stock RROs
         (FrameworksResCommon_Sys, FrameworksResTarget_Vendor) set
         config_defaultRefreshRate to 0 (no global render cap; kept in
         overlay/FrameworksResCommon_Sys); the stock default rate is 120 Hz with
         30/60/90/120/144 user-selectable
         (ro.config.lgsi.default_refreshrate=120,
         ro.config.lgsi.surface_flinger.available_refreshrates=30_60_90_120_144),
         applied by ZUI's own framework.  Keep stock's 0 and make 120 the default
         user peak, so the device defaults to 120 Hz and 144 Hz stays selectable in
         Settings like stock.  This peak is not a stock overlay value: the stock
         overlays only carry 240 (FrameworksResCommon_Sys, FrameworksResTarget_Vendor). -->
    <integer name="config_defaultPeakRefreshRate">120</integer>

    <!-- Color modes (Settings > Display > Colors): Natural, Boosted, Adaptive.
         Adaptive (persist.sys.sf.native_mode=2, system_ext.prop) is the default.
         The stock ZUI list (FrameworksResCommon_Sys) is the same three modes
         followed by the ten vendor modes 256..265; those are not AOSP colour modes
         but raw HAL render intents (SurfaceFlinger's Output.cpp passes the vendor
         value straight to the HWC as the render intent), which only ZUI's own
         Settings/display HAL know how to present, so they are left out here. -->
    <integer-array name="config_availableColorModes">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>1</item>
        <item>3</item>
    </integer-array>

    <!-- Display white balance: the "Adapt color to environment" feature
         (Settings > Display, com.lenovo.settings.display.AutoColorPreferenceController,
         Settings.System "auto_color_temperature").

         Stock does not use this AOSP controller at all.  ZuiDisplayService
         (com.android.server.display, services.jar classes2.dex) observes
         auto_color_temperature through ZuiDisplayService$SettingsObserver and
         registers a SensorEventListener for the qti.sensor.rgb sensor - type
         0x1fa2639 = 33171001, "tcs3408 RGB Sensor" (ams AG), values[0] = ambient
         colour temperature in Kelvin, values[1] = lux (ZUI reads values[3..5] as
         the raw R/G/B channels).  AutoEnvironmentBrightness averages the last 10
         events, maps the ambient CCT to a display CCT with
         AutoColorTempUtil.convertAmbient2Screen (bucket centres 3000/4000/5000/
         6000/7000/8000 K -> 5800/6100/6500/7100/7800/8500/9500, with hysteresis
         on the current bucket; average lux < 50 -> 6500, > 1000 -> 7500), and
         applies a per-panel diagonal gain matrix from getNormalMatrix(cct,
         screenSupplyProduct), animated over 2000 ms, through
         DisplayTransformManager.setColorMatrix(0x41, matrix) to SurfaceFlinger.

         Those stock bucket labels are NOT display colour temperatures.  On the
         TIANMA panel (/proc/cmdline: mdss_dsi_panel_lapis_tianma_nt36532_dsc_3k_video,
         i.e. AutoEnvironmentBrightness.SCREEN_PRODUCT_TIANMA) the table is
         5800:(1.0,0.92,0.81) 6100:(1.0,0.92,0.84) 6500:(1.0,0.94,0.874)
         7100:(1.0,0.95,0.918) 7800:(1.0,0.97,0.966) 8500:(0.998,0.972,1.0)
         9500:(0.95,0.938,1.0): relative to its 7800 bucket the whole stock curve
         moves the white point only between ~5600 K and ~6700 K, leaving R at 1.0
         up to 7800 K and cutting G by <=5% and B by <=16%.  Feeding the labels to
         this controller as display CCTs (the removed config here: 5800..9500) made
         DisplayWhiteBalanceTintController adapt the white point to the real
         Planckian locus at 7800..9500 K, which with the AOSP sRGB primaries is
         R -7%..-15% and G -13%..-23% (peak-white normalised) - i.e. the greenish,
         dimmer picture in the old note and in 57bd89a.

         Two more things made that config misbehave, both fixed here:
         - It kept the AOSP nominal white (D65 coordinates with
           config_displayWhiteBalanceDisplayNominalWhiteCct=6500).  D65 is not on
           the Planckian locus, so "6500" already applied R +4.3% (a pink cast)
           whenever white balance was active.  The nominal white below is the
           6500 K point of ColorSpace.cctToXyz itself, so 6500 K - the CCT the
           stock table leaves untouched - is an exact no-op matrix.
         - It set no config_displayWhiteBalance*Strong* tables, and
           DisplayPowerController enables strong mode for the idle brightness mode
           (DisplayPowerController.setDwbcStrongMode), where a null mapping means
           the raw ambient CCT is used.  The strong tables repeat the normal ones
           so idle mode cannot fall back to the raw sensor value.

         config_displayWhiteBalanceDisplayColorTemperatures is the stock curve
         re-fitted to this controller: for each stock bucket the AOSP matrix at
         the given CCT reproduces the bucket's G and B gains (relative to the
         untouched 7800 bucket) within ~1.5%:
           ambient 2800 -> 5810 (R .931 G .936 B .841; stock rel. G .949 B .839)
           ambient 3500 -> 5934 (R .944 G .948 B .870; stock rel. G .949 B .870)
           ambient 4500 -> 6088 (R .959 G .962 B .906; stock rel. G .969 B .905)
           ambient 5500 -> 6281 (R .978 G .980 B .951; stock rel. G .979 B .950)
           ambient 6500 -> 6500 (no-op; stock's untouched 7800 bucket is
                                 (1.0, 0.97, 0.966), i.e. <=3.4% warmer)
           ambient 7500 -> 6500 (stock 8500 wants B 1.035, above the peak-white
                                 normalisation, so it stays at no-op)
           ambient 8500 -> 6710 (R .974 G .972 B .998; stock rel. R .95 G .967)
         Worst case is the warmest point, relative luminance 0.928 - the same 7%
         as the stock 5800 bucket (0.929) - and the coolest is 0.974, so the panel
         is never yellow, red, green or noticeably dimmer.  The ambient lux rules
         are stock's (neutral below 50 lux, untouched above 1000 lux), the CCT
         clamp keeps the linear extrapolation of the spline inside the fitted
         range, and 250 ms sensor rate / throttler / transition times keep the
         AOSP defaults.

         No config_displayWhiteBalanceDisplayPrimaries override: AOSP falls back
         to its sRGB/D65 primaries (SurfaceFlinger has no panel identification
         data here, "screenPartStatus=UNSUPPORTED"), and the adaptation is
         relative so the primaries only scale the step sizes. -->
    <bool name="config_displayWhiteBalanceAvailable">true</bool>
    <!-- On by default, like the stock setting -->
    <bool name="config_displayWhiteBalanceEnabledDefault">true</bool>
    <string name="config_displayWhiteBalanceColorTemperatureSensorName" translatable="false">qti.sensor.rgb</string>
    <integer name="config_displayWhiteBalanceColorTemperatureMin">5800</integer>
    <integer name="config_displayWhiteBalanceColorTemperatureMax">6750</integer>
    <!-- 6500 K Planckian white of ColorSpace.cctToXyz (x 0.31349, y 0.32366);
         with the AOSP config_displayWhiteBalanceDisplayNominalWhiteCct=6500 this
         makes 6500 K an identity matrix. -->
    <string-array name="config_displayWhiteBalanceDisplayNominalWhite">
        <item>0.968583</item>
        <item>1.000000</item>
        <item>1.121055</item>
    </string-array>
